8. Important Rules
• Do not use arrays as a replacement for the required collections.
• The required collection types must be used for their specified purposes.• Order IDs and menu item IDs must be unique.
• The HashMap must keep every order created, including completed and cancelled orders.
• An order cannot be processed if the kitchen queue is empty.
• An order should not be processed twice.
• An item cannot be added to an order if it does not exist in the menu.
• Completed or cancelled orders should not be modified.
• The program should handle invalid IDs and empty collections without crashing.
• Order status must be represented using the OrderStatus enum.
• Keep the program console-based; no database or graphical interface is required.
9. Collection Design Requirement
At the end of the assignment, the student should be able to explain why each collection was selected:
CollectionWhy is it used?
ArrayListMenu storage and indexed/list-style access.
LinkedListKitchen queue where the first order is processed first.
HashMapPermanent record of every order and fast lookup by order ID.
LinkedHashMapCompleted orders with completion/insertion order preserved.
Focus: This assignment is about understanding and applying Java Collections. Students should be
prepared to explain not only how their code works, but also why ArrayList, LinkedList, HashMap, and
LinkedHashMap were chosen, and how the OrderStatus enum controls the order lifecycle.
